---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import recent from "../data/recent.json";
import line from "../data/line.json";
import families from "../data/families.json";
import register from "../data/people.json";
import ancestors from "../data/ancestors.json";

const blazevic = families.find((f) => f.slug === "blazevic");
const zubrinic = families.find((f) => f.slug === "zubrinic");
---
<Base title="The Blažević Archive" bare
  dek="An evidence-first archive for the family of Hedviga Blažević of Senj.">

  <section class="blk" style="padding-top:1.6rem">
    <figure class="hero">
      <img src={u("/photos/hero.jpg")} width="2000" height="847" fetchpriority="high"
           alt="A stone town on the northern Adriatic beneath a bare karst mountain wall, a fortress on the hill above it and an almost empty harbour below." />
      <div class="cap">
        <div class="kick">Senj · Smokvica Krmpotska · Otočac · Krivi Put · Selce · Karlobag</div>
        <h1>One woman, four families, and the mountain they were planted on.</h1>
        <p class="sub">An evidence-first archive for <strong>Hedviga “Seka” Blažević</strong> —
        born at Senj in 1926, dead at Johannesburg in 2001 — and for the Blaževići, Žubrinići,
        Papići and Prpići who made her.</p>
      </div>
    </figure>
    <p class="credit">Illustration, not a photograph: a generated image of the northern Adriatic
    coast, standing for Senj under the Velebit. Every <em>document</em> reproduced on this site is
    the real scan. <a href={u("/method")}>Why the distinction is laboured here →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This archive begins with one woman — <strong>Hedviga “Seka” Blažević</strong>,
    born at <strong>Senj on 3 August 1926</strong>, married to Ivan Anton Defranceski, and dead at
    <strong>Johannesburg on 30 October 2001</strong> — and works outward into the four families that
    made her: the <strong>Blaževići</strong> of Smokvica Krmpotska, the <strong>Žubrinići</strong> of
    Otočac, the <strong>Papići</strong> of Senj and Selce, and the <strong>Prpići</strong> of Krivi
    Put.</p>
    <p>It reaches <strong>nine generations</strong> and <strong>{ancestors.length} named
    ancestors</strong>, the earliest born about <strong>1717</strong>. Everything here is either
    sourced, reasoned in the open, or labelled as something the family says and the record does not
    yet confirm.</p>
    <p class="cta">
      <a class="btn" href={u("/hedviga")}>Start with Hedviga</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href={u("/direct-line")}>The spine, nine generations</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href={u("/open-questions")}>What is still unproven</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this archive says</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>They are Bunjevci, and that is not a small detail</h3>
        <p>Smokvica Krmpotska and Krivi Put were not old Croatian villages. They were
        <strong>founded</strong> — settled between about <strong>1605 and 1647</strong> by
        <strong>Bunjevci Krmpoćani</strong> who fled the Ottoman advance from Krmpote near Zemunik
        in Dalmatia and were planted in the Senj mountain by the Military Frontier. Two of
        Hedviga's four grandparental lines come out of those settlements.
        <a href={u("/bunjevci")}>The settlement →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>A railway that went somewhere else</h3>
        <p>In <strong>1873</strong> the Karlovac–Rijeka railway <strong>bypassed Senj</strong>, and
        the port that had made the town died with it. The occupations in this family are the
        aftermath: <em>nadničar</em> — day labourer; <em>trhonoša</em> and <em>bremenar</em> —
        load-carrier and porter; <em>mornar</em> — sailor. Then the tobacco factory, then the boats
        out. <a href={u("/senj")}>Senj →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>One name, three spellings, three countries</h3>
        <p><strong>Žubrinić</strong> in Otočac. <strong>Xubrinich</strong> in the Venetian-era
        registers, where <em>X</em> wrote the sound <em>Ž</em>. <strong>Zubrinich</strong> at Port
        Pirie in South Australia, where <a href={u("/port-pirie")}>Ivan Žubrinić landed about 1884</a>
        and every one of his children was registered under the anglicised form. The name changed at
        the border and never changed back. <a href={u("/name")}>The names →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>A brother with two deaths and no grave</h3>
        <p><strong>Oto Blažević</strong> (1923–1945), Hedviga's brother, died at twenty-two. The
        family carries <strong>two irreconcilable accounts</strong> of how — a snakebite, and the
        war in Slavonia — and <a href={u("/grave")}>a photograph of the family grave</a> now
        corroborates the second witness in precise detail, down to who was added to the stone half
        a century later. <a href={u("/oto")}>The brother →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The spine</h2>
    <p class="dek">Eight generations of Blažević men, from Hedviga back to a boy born about 1750 in
    a village that had existed for barely a century.</p>
    <ol class="spine">
      {line.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <span class="g">g{p.gen}</span>
          <a href={u(`/people/${p.slug}`)}>{p.name}</a>
          <span class="yrs">{p.byear || "?"}–{p.dyear || "?"}</span>
          <span class="pl">{p.bornPlace || p.diedPlace || ""}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="note"><strong>Generation three is now documented.</strong> The
    <a href={u("/marriage-1920")}>Senj marriage register of 8 December 1920</a> names Ljubomir's
    parents as <strong>Juraj Blažević and Tereza <em>r.</em> Žubrinić</strong> — closing the join
    this archive was built around. Juraj himself is still undated, and nothing yet links him to
    Toma above. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>See the open questions →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Recently found</div>
    <h2>What has moved lately</h2>
    <p class="lede">An archive that never changes is not being worked on. Most of what follows is
    this one becoming <strong>less</strong> certain rather than more — which is what happens when
    somebody actually reads the registers behind a family tree.</p>
    <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <tbody>{recent.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="mono dim" style="width:8.5rem">{r.d}</td>
          <td><a href={u(r.h)}><strong>{r.t}</strong></a>
            <div class="dek" style="margin-top:.2rem" set:html={r.s} /></td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><a class="btn" href={u("/changed")}>Everything this archive got
    wrong →</a> <a class="btn" href={u("/searched")}>What has been searched →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>By the numbers</h2>
    <div class="grid three">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{register.length.toLocaleString()}</div><div class="l">people carrying one of the
        eight surnames, published</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{ancestors.length}</div><div class="l">named ancestors of Hedviga, across nine generations</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{blazevic.earliest}</div><div class="l">earliest Blažević birth year in the
        tree</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{zubrinic.count}</div><div class="l">Žubrinići — of a surname held by about
        <strong>312 people on earth</strong></div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">35</div><div class="l">people born at one address: <em>Krivi Put 136</em></div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">2,034</div><div class="l">people judged living and withheld from this build
        entirely</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
